factors, while Smith reduction is available after localization \cite[Theorems~1.2.30 and~1.7.2]{Cohen2000}; the need for ideal rather than scalar Smith data over rings of algebraic integers is classical \cite{NewmanThompson1991}. Recent work of Van Werde expresses exact integrality probabilities for orthogonal conjugation of general symmetric integral matrices through Smith ideals of a scaled orthogonal matrix \cite[Theorem~1.3, Theorem~2.3 and Lemmas~2.5--2.7]{VanWerde2026}, and later constrains orthogonal conjugators for a fixed symmetric matrix by the level ideal and related modules \cite[Theorem~2.1 and Theorems~2.8--2.9]{VanWerdeCospectral2026}. The present problem has a different axis: $Q$ is fixed, the matrix being conjugated is restricted to the diagonal family $\operatorname{diag}(\lambda)$, and the objective is to determine the entire module $L_Q$ and the ideal-valued structure of $M_Q$.

Theorem~\ref{thm:global-obstruction} gives the global obstruction. It removes the common scalar shift, identifies a difference-coordinate presentation, proves $\operatorname{Ann}_R(M_Q)=\mathbf d_Q^2$, and writes $M_Q$ as a direct sum of cyclic modules $R/\mathbf e_i$ with $\mathbf e_1=\mathbf d_Q^2$; the same ideals are recovered from determinantal ideals of the rank-one symmetric matrices $q_iq_i^T$. Theorem~\ref{thm:low-order-determinantal} then gives exact rank-two and rank-three determinantal identities for such rank-one symmetric columns, in the broader context of determinantal ideals of symmetric and exterior powers studied by Bruns and Vasconcelos \cite{BrunsVasconcelos2003}; Corollary~\ref{cor:low-obstruction} converts them into explicit low obstruction layers.

Corollary~\ref{cor:counting-reconstruction} gives finite counting profiles that recover the obstruction module, and Corollary~\ref{cor:dimension-four} records the dimension-four Hodge-duality specialization. In dimension three, Theorem~\ref{thm:dimension-three} determines the complete module and Corollary~\ref{cor:minus-one-square} gives the resulting residue-field restriction. Section~\ref{sec:householder} treats Householder reflections under $\operatorname{char}K\ne2$: Theorem~\ref{thm:householder-obstruction} determines the local and global obstruction, Corollary~\ref{cor:householder-fitting-bounds} gives its Fitting ideal and sharp cardinality bounds, while Theorem~\ref{thm:householder-class} and Corollary~\ref{cor:class-realization} determine the attainable denominator classes modulo the subgroup generated by primes above $2$. Finally, Example~\ref{ex:nonprincipal-obstruction} over $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-5}]$ produces a genuinely nonprincipal cyclic factor, showing why scalar Smith data are insufficient beyond principal ideal domains. Precise comparisons with the closest neighboring results are placed next to the corresponding theorems below.

Section~\ref{sec:obstruction} develops the global and low-order obstruction theory, Section~\ref{sec:dimension-three} treats dimension three, Section~\ref{sec:householder} treats Householder reflections and class-group realization, and Section~\ref{sec:nonprincipal} gives the nonprincipal example.

\section{Ideal-Valued Obstruction and Determinantal Recovery}\label{sec:obstruction}
We retain the notation $L_Q$, $M_Q$, and $\mathbf d_Q$ from the Introduction. A \emph{fractional ideal} is a nonzero finitely generated $R$-submodule of $K$ \cite[Definition~1.2.2]{Cohen2000}. For a nonzero prime ideal $\mathbf p$, the localization $R_{\mathbf p}$ is a discrete valuation ring (DVR) \cite[Ch.~I, \S11, Proposition~11.5]{Neukirch1999}; write $v_{\mathbf p}$ for its normalized valuation, set $v_{\mathbf p}(0)=+\infty$, and write $\kappa(\mathbf p)=R_{\mathbf p}/\mathbf pR_{\mathbf p}\cong R/\mathbf p$. For a nonzero fractional ideal $\mathbf a$, $v_{\mathbf p}(\mathbf a)$ denotes the exponent of $\mathbf p$ in its prime-ideal factorization, and $\mathbf p\mid\mathbf a$ means $v_{\mathbf p}(\mathbf a)>0$. For fractional ideals $\mathbf a